    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Syrian refugee children ages 6-10 years old newly resettled in Lebanon are facing many challenges integrating into their new schools. They face bullying from Lebanese children, trauma triggers that interfere with their learning because they lack coping skills, and they are immersed in unstable conditions living in temporary resettlement tents. The low-income Lebanese public school children do not understand the circumstances of the Syrian children and to a certain extent cannot empathize with their situation. Their bullying comments are born of a fear of limited resources being depleted, and jealousy toward the apparent aid the Syrian children are receiving. Many Syrian children are displaying classroom-disruptive symptoms of trauma such as squatting under desks when they feel stressed. The Syrian children need access to first response psychiatric intervention: effective tools to manage their stress and trauma via relaxation techniques, and perspectives on hope for the future. The Lebanese children need help contextualizing the plight of the Syrian children and understanding their own roles in building a cooperative environment for the school newcomers. I aimed to design a self-contained tech product that hosts pre-recorded audio tracks based on CBITS, PRACTICE, and trauma-focused therapy methods. These audio tracks are meant to serve as first-response trauma management aid until the Syrian children can receive clinical psychiatric care, and also help the Lebanese children empathize and cope with the stress of changes in their country. Beity can be solar powered, or run on a battery rechargeable with a cell phone charger. The plexiglass house-shaped “home” is mobile, and accessible in a resettlement camp or school setting. A child can listen to the content individually with headphones, or 3-4 children can crowd around the device together to listen to the audio through the built-in speaker. Due to the fragile nature of the population I designed for (trauma-exposed Syrian children), my learner studies were conducted with Syrian-American 6-8 year old children who know about the conflict in Syria and actively attend protests and vigils with their parents. Design was also informed by psychological professionals: Lebanese school administration, social workers, and Stanford Medicine children’s stress and trauma psychiatrist Dr. Hilit Kletter. My plan for piloting Beity in a refugee camp in Lebanon includes distributing –Pre and –Posttest surveys to assess children’s emotion regulation skills, hopeful perspectives, and built empathy. I would like medical professionals to help me pilot the content so that if flaws arise or the content triggers anxiety or panic, professionals are immediately on hand to intervene.",
